<blockquote>
<p>Just because it's in an article doesn't make it part of the lore.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>HAHAHAHAHAHA</p>
<p>Oh. You're serious?</p>
<p>Lore is created as successful works are created. One in the same. This is part of the lore, as others have already helpfully lined out.</p>
<p>I'll have to bring this out a step further though: This kind of &quot;Thou shalt not&quot; stance is the type of bullshit that inhibits creativity, experimentation, and expression.</p>
<p>If someone has a different idea for how things operate<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-480486-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-480486-1')">1</a></sup>, so long as it is <strong>internally consistent</strong>, more power to them. All that matters is if the story itself is entertaining. We don't grow or evolve by writing samey shit en masse.</p>
<p>Perfectly acceptable reason to downvote, if indeed it's truly that abhorrent to name something slightly different, but it just looks asinine to maintain that everything should neatly fall in line with everything else that exists on the mainlist.</p>
<p>Stamping one's feet and pointing to a site resource as if it's the end-all be-all of what someone is allowed to do is foolish.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-480486-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-480486-1')">1</a>. Granted, this is an incredibly minor difference - I'm of the opinion that we've not nearly enough works that display a vastly different Foundation, say, such as that in Broken Masquerade, unconcerned with maintaining the veil.</div>
</div>

